// EXPECT: [E011]
// EXPECT-TEXT: this loop is not provably terminating
// I.85: a loop's test ends it only if its ELSE branch leaves the loop. In `while i < 10 or b` the
// header's else goes on to test `b`, inside the loop, so `i < 10` failing does not end it. The
// termination rule read the header's test as "the loop continues while this holds", counted the
// store of 10 as progress (it makes that test false), and proved this loop terminating; with `b`
// true it runs forever. No arithmetic, so nothing but termination can refuse it (Handwriting's
// reproducer). B1 and the guard-counter rule had the same assumption:
// tests/vra/or_guard_trip_count_fail.ln and or_guard_counter_fail.ln.
func spin(b bool) i32 {
    var i i32 = 0
    while i < 10 or b {
        i = 10
    }
    return i
}

func main() i32 {
    return spin(false) - 10
}
